A||||10||Bazooka||Fiero Ideosycracies||||1||
Z||000000||Bazooka||11-17-2001||10:53 PM||||    I would like to hear about some of the things found on the Fiero that are unique and unusual from most other cars. The fact of having a Mid-Engine design does present many new subsystems, but even the plastic body, space frame, and suspension are different. The "wheel well access" for belts and timing are also not common on rear wheel drives. The other day when I popped the front hood, I stopped to look at the headlight mechanisms and I had a hard time identifying the components as to their fuction. It seems like everything on these cars is "one of a kind". ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||11-18-2001||03:29 AM||||Actually, it's nothing like that at all, especially for the 84-87 cars.<P>GM has always been very big on sharing parts across multiple platforms.  For example, the headlight motors shared a large amount of their parts with the headlight motors on Corvettes and Camaros/Firebirds from the same era.  Also, things like working on the belts from the wheel well...these tricks are the same as on any FWD car, just in the back <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>The car is really not a whole lot different than any other car if you look at it as a purely component/mechanical issue.  I'd never worked on a Fiero before I bought mine, but haven't really had to learn anything new to work on it.  I guess the biggest ideosyncracy with it is engine removal.  You can remove the engine from the top if you want to, but the car is really designed to have the engine removed from the bottom.<P>A lot of people immediately think the car is hard to work on because it's mid/rear engined, fuel tank is in the middle of the car, etc...but if you think about it, it's really not that much different than anything else.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bazooka||11-18-2001||10:57 AM||||Well said! But, looking at things like purging the cooling system properly is something that is exclusive to the Fiero. The power distribution thru the bulkhead is unique. So is the routing of the tubing and hoses along the bottom sides of the car. The positive offsets of the wheels have given headaches to many people buying new ones. Even the outstandingly cheap brake rotor thickness is an item I'lle never know why GM overlooked. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||VeeSixGeeTee||11-18-2001||11:52 AM||||right on!..I can't think of anything else that is unique.....2 seats  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mikey<BR>85 GT 4sp..Taking a six month nap!<BR>AIM: KilleredGT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bazooka||11-18-2001||01:42 PM||||The Sheet Molded Compound (SMC) is quite different as you may soon find out if you have bodywork. The Cooling Fan on the Passengers side trunk for blowing air over the coil, etc., is unique. True that many parts have been used elsewhere, like the Chevette suspension. I really appreciate your inputs here...Thanks a lot!<BR>*********************************<BR>[QUOTE]Originally posted by VeeSixGeeTee:<BR>right on!..I can't think of anything else that is unique.....2 seats   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||11-18-2001||02:32 PM||||-Location of fuel door lever.<BR>-Why the hell is the v6 butted up to the firewall, and has a good 8-10" of space to the rear??! They could have made the cradle EVER so slightly different... AND It'D BE 5x EASIER TO CHANGE THE FRONT PLUGS<BR>-Whoever designed the front compartment had turette's syndrome... Love them Speed Holes that open up when you hit 170  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>-T125 is possibly the nastiest tranny I've ever used.. It's a shifting waterbed. Ick! Must get shift-kit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>-Doors are too hard to dissasemble.<BR>-Donut tire just looks GAY. *stares out window and curses all screws*<BR>-The heat sheilds in the engine compartment will eat tools. NEVER drop one. It'll NEVER hit the ground, and you WON'T find it. But you'll hear it rattling over every bump<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000006||cadaver||11-18-2001||09:47 PM||||<BR>Purging the cooling system is not exclusivly a fiero thing. I had a jap car that had a port on the housing for doing this.<BR>Power distribution through the bulkhead?<BR>Oh like the wires running through the firewall on any other car is any different?<BR>Tube routing?<BR>How else are you going to get the coolant back there?<BR>Positive offset?||||reg||1
Z||000007||artherd||11-18-2001||10:37 PM||||The engines drop like flies after a nuculear event, but the (Getrag) trannys are bulletproof with a nearly perfect record?<P>The look of 'oil stop' attendants upon opening the hood and not finding an engine!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000008||rogergarrison||11-18-2001||10:54 PM||||My only 'real' problem is that everywhere you turn, they used cheap/inferior bolts and studs. Good example is exhaust manifolds. I just replaced my water pump this weekend and guess what........yep more broken bolts. Ive never had so many that break off so ez working on any other car, some even 40 years old. It makes most engine repairs scary to say the least. All 1 hour jobs turn into major catastrophes.||||reg||1
